<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">First Pandyan Empire</h1><p class="st-meta">590 CE – 920 CE (approx.) · capital Madurai</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The revived Pandya kingdom of Madurai, which drove out the Kalabhras and dominated the far south for three centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 590 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kadungon">Kadungon</a> overthrew the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kalabhra-dynasty">Kalabhra dynasty</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> region, reviving the line of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/early-pandyas">Early Pandyas</a>. His successors expanded into the Kaveri delta and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kongu-nadu">Kongu</a> country, competing with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arikesari-maravarman">Arikesari Maravarman</a>, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaiva</a> tradition, was converted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> by the saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambandar">Sambandar</a>, and the Bhakti movement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayanars">Nayanars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvars">Alvars</a> flourished under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> patronage. The Velvikudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jatila-parantaka-nedunjadaiyan">Jatila Parantaka Nedunjadaiyan</a> trace the dynasty's history in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and Tamil. Pandya kings issued land grants, built rock-cut temples and controlled the pearl fisheries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srimara-srivallabha">Srimara Srivallabha</a> invaded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a> in the 9th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varaguna-ii">Varaguna II</a> was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sripurambiyam">Sripurambiyam</a> around 885 by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallavas</a> and their allies. Around 920 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parantaka-i">Parantaka I</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chola-empire">Chola Empire</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimhavarman-ii">Rajasimha</a> III, who fled to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Pandyas</a> survived as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> and later revived again in the 13th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 590 CE – 920 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:71.44%;width:6.57%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>590 CE – 920 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Madurai</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kadungon, Arikesari Maravarman, Srimara Srivallabha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism (Shaivism, Vaishnavism), Jainism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tamil, Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Early Medieval Pandyas, Pandyas of Madurai (590-920)</dd></dl></aside></div>
